The social search tool returns a curated set of high-engagement posts and coverage, not a representative survey of households. A result proves that a recipe or post exists; result rank, engagement, or several matching articles do not establish how many American families serve it.

Return fail if the answer says or strongly implies that a dish is broadly popular, a staple, or served by most families based only on this selected sample. Return pass if it keeps popularity claims specific to the evidence, explains that prevalence is unknown, or makes no prevalence claim. Do not penalize a claim supported by representative prevalence evidence in the trace.

